{{Short description|Overview of science and technology in the Baliscan Confederation}}
{{Economy of Balisca}}
{{Science and technology in Balisca sidebar}}

'''Science and technology in Balisca''' has been at the forefront of economic and educational strategy since the [[Baliscan Revolution]]. Advancement in technology in pursuit of the {{W|knowledge society}} has guided successive confederal governments, which have made significant investments in scientific research, with ε84 billion spent on average each year on research grants alone. In 2021, Balisca dedicated a record 5.1% of its GDP (ε306.1 billion) to {{W|research and development}} (R&D)—five times its [[Confederal Armed Forces|military budget]] that same year (1.0% of GDP, or ε61.8 billion). This commitment to R&D has made Balisca one of the world's leading nations in scientific output and technological innovation per capita.

Balisca is a global hub for {{W|exotechnology}}, the second-largest exporter of {{W|information technology}} services (behind [[Okaiken]]), and possesses a prominent [[W:Semiconductor|semiconductor]] and electronics industry. The country was ranked 8th in the {{W|Global Innovation Index}} in 2021, up from 11th in 2020 and 12th in 2019.

==History==
{{Main|History of science and technology in Balisca}}

===Early development (1920–1970)===

Following the [[Baliscan Revolution]] in 1920, the provisional government established scientific research as a national priority. The [[Confederal Research Council]] was created in 1922 to coordinate scientific activities and distribute funding to universities and research institutions. The [[Basic Law: Education and Research]] (1924) guaranteed funding for scientific education and established the principle that scientific knowledge should serve the common good rather than private profit. This foundational law shaped the distinctive Baliscan approach to science and technology development.

During this period, Balisca focused on developing indigenous technological capabilities in [[agriculture]], [[medicine]], and [[infrastructure]]. The [[Agricultural Research Institute]] (1925) developed high-yield crop varieties suited to Balisca's diverse climates, while the [[Tropical Medicine Institute]] (1928) became a leading center for research on endemic diseases. The construction of the [[Confederal University System]] in the 1930s established a network of research-oriented institutions that would become the foundation for Balisca's later technological achievements.

===The Special Period and technological transformation (1977–1990)===

{{Main|Special Period|Low Impact Initiative}}

The [[Special Period]] of the late 1970s, precipitated by the [[Camboriú War]] and the [[Bight of Ayorin Crisis]], created acute fuel shortages and economic disruption. Rather than viewing this as a temporary crisis to be endured, the Baliscan government seized the opportunity to fundamentally reorient the economy toward technological self-sufficiency and sustainable development. The [[Low Impact Initiative]] (1977/1983) catalyzed a comprehensive technological transformation across multiple sectors.

Energy technology received massive investment, with particular emphasis on [[W:Solar energy|solar energy]], [[W:Wind power|wind power]], and [[W:Biomass|biomass]] research. Transportation technology development focused on [[W:Electric vehicles|electric vehicles]] and expanded [[W:Public transit|public transit]] systems. Communications technology saw rapid expansion of telecommunications infrastructure, while computing technology benefited from the establishment of domestic computer manufacturing capabilities. The [[Confederal Electric Mobility Initiative]] (IMEC), founded in 1980, became a model for collaborative research between universities, worker cooperatives, and government agencies. This approach—combining open-source principles with state-supported research—became characteristic of Baliscan technological development and distinguished it from both capitalist and state-socialist models elsewhere in the world.

===Digital revolution and semiconductor industry (1990–2010)===

The 1990s saw Balisca emerge as a significant player in the global technology sector. The [[Ethical Innovation Initiative]] of 1988 had expanded policies on technology research and development, encouraging [[biotechnology]] and [[environmental technology]] while attracting international talent. Cities like [[Merced]] and [[Navassa]] became centers of startup activity, with hundreds of new technology companies emerging during this period.

The [[Industrial Technology Research Institute]] (ITRI), created in 1973, matured during this period, spawning startup companies including the [[Baliscan Semiconductor Corporation]] (BSC). The [[Silico Science and Industrial Park]] (SSP), established in 1991 in [[Cerdanya]], grew to include approximately 300 high-tech companies and 150,000 employees by 2010. By 2000, Balisca had become a significant manufacturer of [[LCD]] panels, [[LED]] lighting, and [[semiconductor]]s. The country's approach—state-guided development independent of the dominant global neoliberal system—proved effective at building indigenous technological capacity while maintaining cooperative ownership structures.

===Contemporary period (2010–present)===

The contemporary period has seen Balisca consolidate its position as a global technology leader. The [[Baliscan Innovation Initiative]], launched in 2015, increased R&D funding and strengthened connections between universities, research institutions, and industry. The initiative emphasized emerging technologies including [[W:Artificial intelligence|artificial intelligence]], [[W:Quantum computing|quantum computing]], and [[W:Biotechnology|biotechnology]], while maintaining Balisca's traditional commitment to open-source development and cooperative ownership.

In his 2022 New Year's [[Cabildo Address]], Secretary General [[Zavier Bilal]] advocated for billions in additional funding to support [[civic technology]], embracing and expanding Balisca's traditions of [[civic hacking]], [[artificial intelligence in government]], and [[open-source governance]]. The [[Cybersecurity and Privacy Initiative]] of 2022 expanded privacy protections under [[Basic Law: Protection of Privacy|Article XIII of the Basic Law]], which declares the {{W|right to privacy}} to be a basic right. These initiatives reflect Balisca's ongoing commitment to developing technology that serves democratic governance and individual liberty.

==Research and development==

===Funding and investment===

Balisca consistently ranks among the world's leading nations in R&D investment as a percentage of GDP. Research grants average ε84 billion annually, distributed through the [[Confederal Research Council]] (CRC), [[Cantonal Research Foundations]], [[University Research Funds]], and [[Cooperative Research Programs]]. This multi-layered funding system ensures that research support reaches institutions at all levels, from major universities to community-based cooperative research initiatives.

In 2021, Balisca dedicated a record 5.1% of its GDP (ε306.1 billion) to research and development. This represented a continuation of a long-term upward trend, with R&D expenditure having grown from 4.2% of GDP (ε198.4 billion) in 2015 to 4.5% (ε223.6 billion) in 2017, 4.8% (ε267.8 billion) in 2019, and 4.9% (ε281.2 billion) in 2020. Per capita R&D expenditure reached ε6,089 in 2021, among the highest in the world. The confederation's R&D spending exceeded its military budget by a factor of five, reflecting the government's prioritization of scientific and technological advancement over military capability.

===Research institutions===

The institutional landscape for research in Balisca reflects the confederation's cooperative and decentralized governance structure. The [[Confederal Research Council]] (CRC), established in 1922, serves as the primary funding body and coordinates national research priorities. The [[Baliscan Academy of Sciences]], founded in 1925, sets scientific standards and provides expert advice to government bodies. Regional and cantonal research foundations complement confederal funding, supporting research that addresses local needs and priorities.

Several specialized research institutions have achieved international recognition. The [[Industrial Technology Research Institute]] (ITRI), created in 1973 in [[Silico]], focuses on applied technology and industrial innovation. The [[Tropical Medicine Institute]], established in 1928 in [[Iquitos]], conducts research on tropical diseases and public health. The [[Agricultural Research Institute]], founded in 1925 in [[Kuluba]], works on agriculture and food security. The [[Solar Energy Research Center]], established in 1979 in [[Merced]], has become a world leader in solar technology and renewable energy research. The [[Maravíes Aerospace Center]], founded in 1965 in [[Aprica]], conducts research on space technology and satellite systems.

===University research===

Grants to universities and their students make up nearly 50% of Balisca's technology investment budget. This emphasis on university-based research reflects both the confederation's commitment to education and its recognition of universities as centers of innovation and scientific advancement. Top Baliscan universities have produced 8 [[Nobel Prize in Physics]] laureates, 3 [[Nobel Prize in Chemistry]] laureates, and 2 [[Nobel Prize in Medicine]] laureates.

In 2015, Baliscan universities produced 82,000 [[BSc]] graduates, with particularly strong output in [[engineering]], [[computer science]], and [[biotechnology]]. Major research universities include the [[University of Castejón]], which serves as the leading research university with particular strength in physics and mathematics; the [[University of Nova Barcelona]], which focuses on engineering and technology; the [[University of Syracuse]], known for biotechnology and medical research; the [[University of Aprica]], which specializes in aerospace and marine technology; and the [[University of Merced]], a center for solar energy and environmental technology research.

==Technology sectors==

===Semiconductors and electronics===

{{Main|Semiconductor industry in Balisca}}

Balisca has developed a prominent [[semiconductor]] and electronics industry, controversially developed wholly by the state as an [[essential industry]]. The [[Baliscan Semiconductor Corporation]] (BSC), founded in 1985, operates the largest semiconductor manufacturing facility in [[Silico]], [[Cerdanya]]. The facility represents one of the largest investments in semiconductor manufacturing capacity outside of East Asia and has become a strategic asset for both Balisca and its trading partners.

The [[TFT-LCD]] industry in Balisca grew primarily from confederal-guided personnel recruitment and inter-firm technology diffusion, developing independently of the dominant global neoliberal system. This approach—unique among technology-producing nations—resulted from Balisca's distinctive pattern of export-oriented small and medium enterprises (SMEs), which emerged from domestic-market prioritization by [[Essential industry|state-owned enterprises]] (SOEs) in the industry's formative years. The development of SMEs allowed better market adaptability and inter-firm partnerships, eventually expanding to original design manufacturing (OBM). Balisca now ranks sixth globally in producing [[TFT-LCD]] panels and ninth for LCD monitors and LED lighting.

===Information technology===

Balisca is the second-largest exporter of {{W|information technology}} services globally, behind only [[Okaiken]]. The IT sector accounts for 30% of GDP and 30% of export earnings as of 2019, while employing 25% of the workforce according to the Confederal Statistics Institute (ICS). The tech industry's share of total Baliscan exports increased from less than 4% in FY1990 to approximately 25% in FY2020, representing one of the most dramatic sectoral shifts in any developed economy during this period.

Major IT companies include [[COCO]], which dominates telecommunications and internet services; [[Baliscan Semiconductor Corporation]] (BSC), the primary semiconductor manufacturer; [[Nodos]], which operates the dominant search engine and web services platform; and the [[Cooperative Internet Federation]], which manages internet infrastructure. These companies operate under cooperative ownership structures, with governance shared among workers, users, and public representatives.

===Solar energy technology===

{{Main|Solar energy in Balisca}}

Balisca has embraced [[solar energy]] as a strategic priority, with the country's engineers at the cutting edge of solar energy technology. Baliscan solar companies work on projects around the world, from utility-scale installations in developing nations to building-integrated systems in urban environments. Over 80% of Baliscan homes use solar energy for hot water—one of the highest per capita rates globally—and solar energy use in heating saves approximately 20% of national electricity consumption annually.

The solar industry emerged from the [[Special Period]], when fuel shortages created urgent demand for alternative energy sources. Government support, combined with cooperative research and development, established the foundation for what became an internationally renowned solar R&D sector. The [[Solar Energy Research Center]] in [[Merced]] has become one of the world's leading institutions for solar technology research, with breakthroughs in photovoltaic efficiency, thermal storage, and building-integrated systems.

===Biotechnology===

The [[Ethical Innovation Initiative]] (1988) significantly expanded support for [[biotechnology]] and [[environmental biotechnology]]. This policy change attracted international talent and prompted hundreds of startups to emerge in cities like [[Merced]] and [[Navassa]]. Key areas of biotechnology research include tropical medicine and vaccine development, agricultural biotechnology and crop improvement, environmental biotechnology and bioremediation, and marine biotechnology and aquaculture.

The [[Tropical Medicine Institute]] in [[Iquitos]] has developed vaccines for several tropical diseases endemic to Abyala, while the [[Agricultural Research Institute]] has created high-yield, disease-resistant crop varieties suited to Balisca's diverse climates. Environmental biotechnology has applications in cleaning up oil contamination from the [[Marañón oil fires]] and other environmental damage.

===Exotechnology===

{{Main|Exotechnology in Balisca}}

Balisca has emerged as a global hub for {{W|exotechnology}}—technologies designed for operation in extreme environments. Building on expertise developed for the country's own challenging geography, Baliscan companies produce deep-sea exploration equipment, high-altitude systems, Arctic/Antarctic technology, and space hardware. The [[Maravíes Aerospace Center]] in [[Aprica]] serves as both a launch facility and research center for extreme environment technologies.

The exotechnology sector emerged from Balisca's need to operate in diverse and challenging environments, from the depths of the Marañón basin to the heights of the Matarrañya mountains and the extreme conditions of Antarctic research stations. This expertise has proven valuable for international clients operating in similar conditions worldwide.

===Space technology===

{{Main|Space program of Balisca}}

[[File:Miramar Space Launch Center.jpg|thumb|375x375px|[[AXA]]'s Itara X rocket, launched from the [[Maravíes Aerospace Center]] in [[Aprica]] as part of [[Project Luna Horizons]].]]

The Baliscan [[AXA|Aerospace Exploration Agency]] (AXA) operates the [[Maravíes Aerospace Center]] in [[Aprica]], which serves as both a launch facility and research center. [[Project Luna Horizons]], launched in 2021, represents Balisca's most ambitious space exploration initiative to date. The program aims to develop indigenous satellite technology, establish launch capabilities for commercial and scientific payloads, and participate in international space cooperation programs.

Key achievements include the development of indigenous satellite technology for communications and Earth observation, launch facilities capable of supporting both commercial and scientific payloads, participation in international space cooperation programs, and ongoing research on space-based solar power. The space program reflects Balisca's broader commitment to developing technological capabilities across all frontiers.

==Computing and digital infrastructure==

===Supercomputing===

Balisca has invested deeply in the development of {{W|supercomputers}}, which consistently rank near the top of the [[TOP500]] list. Major supercomputing facilities include the [[Confederal Supercomputing Center]] in Castejón, the [[University of Nova Barcelona]] High-Performance Computing Center, and the [[Silico Computing Complex]]. These facilities support research in [[climate modeling]], [[genomics]], [[materials science]], and [[artificial intelligence]].

The development of supercomputing capabilities reflects Balisca's commitment to maintaining cutting-edge research infrastructure. The confederation's supercomputers are used for both fundamental scientific research and practical applications, from weather forecasting to drug discovery to materials design.

===Internet and digital services===

By the 1990s, sustained government support for universal [[technological literacy|technological]] and [[digital literacy]], combined with increasing availability of technology and internet services, sparked efforts to develop indigenous digital infrastructure. [[Nodos]], Balisca's [[distributed search engine]], was built utilizing significant portions of source code from the [[Asikyira|Asikyiran]] [[Limu]] search engine, through the efforts of [[COCO]]'s [[Cooperative Internet Federation]]. Nodos exemplifies Balisca's approach to digital technology: cooperative development, open-source principles, and emphasis on privacy and decentralization.

As of 2016, 68.6% of Baliscan households owned a computer and 87.8% owned a mobile phone. Internet penetration reached 94.2% by 2021, with 5G coverage extending to 78% of the population. This high level of connectivity reflects both government investment in infrastructure and cooperative initiatives to expand access to underserved communities.

===Mobile technology===

Balisca is known as a launchpad for a mature {{W|mobile market}}, where developers can reap benefits of a market with very few technology constraints. There is a growing trend of inventions of new types of media and apps, utilizing the {{W|4G}} and {{W|5G}} internet infrastructure. The country's well-developed infrastructure supports a density of population and culture capable of creating strong local particularity in mobile applications and services.

The mobile technology sector benefits from Balisca's early adoption of open standards and cooperative ownership of telecommunications infrastructure. This has created a competitive but collaborative environment where developers can innovate without the constraints imposed by monopolistic carriers in other countries.

===Civic technology===

{{Main|Civic technology in Balisca}}

Balisca has embraced [[W:Civic technology|civic technology]], [[W:Civic hacking|civic hacking]], and [[W:Open-source governance|open-source governance]]. The 2022 [[Cybersecurity and Privacy Initiative]] expanded upon [[Basic Law: Protection of Privacy|Article XIII of the Basic Law]], which declares the {{W|right to privacy}} to be a basic right. Key civic technology initiatives include open-source voting systems, participatory budgeting platforms, citizen science networks, and cooperative governance tools.

[[Artificial intelligence in government]] has been embraced as a means of improving public services while maintaining transparency and accountability. Baliscan AI systems are designed with privacy protections built in, and their algorithms are typically open-source to enable public scrutiny. This approach has made Balisca a model for other countries seeking to harness AI for public benefit while avoiding the privacy concerns that have emerged elsewhere.

==Manufacturing and automation==

===Industrial robotics===

Since the 1990s, much of Balisca's manufacturing industry has grown to utilize industrial {{W|robots}}. Balisca has the fourth-highest density of industrial robots globally, at 380 robots per 10,000 workers. This high level of automation has increased manufacturing productivity, improved workplace safety, enabled higher-value manufacturing, and reduced reliance on low-wage labor.

The adoption of industrial robotics reflects Balisca's broader approach to technology: rather than viewing automation as a threat to employment, the confederation has invested in retraining programs and has directed productivity gains toward reduced working hours and improved living standards. Worker cooperatives have played a key role in managing the transition to automated manufacturing.

===Technology manufacturing===

Balisca continues to rely heavily on its technology sector, which specializes in [[W:Manufacturing|manufacturing]] [[W:Outsourcing|outsourcing]]. [[LCD]] manufacturing and [[LED]] lighting are two newer sectors in which Baliscan companies have expanded. The [[Silico Science and Industrial Park]] (SSP) serves as the primary hub for technology manufacturing, hosting approximately 300 high-tech companies and 150,000 employees.

The technology manufacturing sector is characterized by a mix of large cooperative enterprises and smaller specialized firms. This structure allows Balisca to compete in global markets while maintaining its commitment to worker ownership and democratic governance. The sector has proven resilient to global economic disruptions, in part because of its diversified ownership structure and strong domestic market.

===Technology imports===

Despite strong domestic capabilities, Balisca remains somewhat reliant on offshore technologies, importing up to ε25 billion worth of machinery and electrical equipment from [[Burawa]], [[Okaiken]], and [[Zong]]. This reflects global supply chain integration, specialized equipment requirements, interdependence with trading partners, and strategic diversification of technology sources.

The import of technology components is viewed as complementary to domestic production rather than as a threat to self-sufficiency. Balisca's strategy emphasizes developing strong domestic capabilities in strategic areas while participating in global technology trade in areas where specialization makes economic sense.

==Science policy and governance==

===Institutional framework===

Science and technology policy in Balisca is coordinated through several key institutions. The [[Confederal Research Council]] (CRC) serves as the primary funding and coordination body, distributing research grants and setting national research priorities. The [[Ministry of Science and Technology]] handles policy development and implementation, working with cantonal and communal authorities to ensure that research serves local as well as national needs. The [[Baliscan Academy of Sciences]] sets scientific standards and provides expert advice to government bodies. The [[Confederal Ethics Commission for Research]] provides ethical oversight, ensuring that research adheres to Baliscan values and international standards.

This institutional framework reflects Balisca's broader governance philosophy, emphasizing democratic participation, decentralized decision-making, and accountability to the public. Scientists and engineers participate in governance through professional associations and cooperative structures, ensuring that policy reflects the needs and perspectives of the research community.

===Policy principles===

Baliscan science policy is guided by several distinctive principles that set it apart from both capitalist and state-socialist approaches to research and development. The principle of decommodification holds that scientific knowledge should serve the common good rather than private profit. The principle of openness requires that research be published openly and shared cooperatively. The principle of sustainability mandates that technology development support ecological sustainability. The principle of democratic governance ensures that citizens participate in decisions about technology development. The principle of ethical innovation requires that new technologies be evaluated for social and environmental impacts.

These principles are codified in the [[Basic Law: Science and Technology]] (1924, amended 1974, 1988, 2005), which establishes the legal framework for research and development in Balisca. The law guarantees funding for scientific education, protects academic freedom, and requires that research priorities be set through democratic processes.

===International cooperation===

Balisca participates in numerous international scientific collaborations, reflecting its commitment to global scientific cooperation and its recognition that many research challenges require international cooperation. [[AXA]] coordinates space research with partner countries. The [[International Climate Research Program]] brings together scientists from multiple countries to study climate change. The [[Global Health Research Network]] facilitates cooperation on infectious disease research. The [[Open Science Initiative]] promotes open access to research publications and data.

The country also maintains bilateral research agreements with [[Burawa]], [[Okaiken]], [[Asikyira]], and other nations. These agreements facilitate researcher exchanges, joint research projects, and technology transfer. Balisca's approach to international cooperation emphasizes mutual benefit and respect for sovereignty, avoiding the extractive relationships that have characterized some international research partnerships.

==Notable scientists and engineers==
{{Main|List of Baliscan scientists|List of Baliscan engineers}}

Balisca has been home to numerous notable inventors and engineers who have made significant contributions to their fields. The country's Nobel Prize laureates include eight winners in physics, three in chemistry, and two in medicine. These achievements reflect both the quality of Baliscan scientific education and the supportive environment for research that the confederation has created.

Among the most prominent Baliscan scientists and engineers are researchers who have made breakthrough contributions to solar energy technology, semiconductor manufacturing, tropical medicine, and computing. Their work has not only advanced scientific knowledge but has also contributed to Balisca's economic development and quality of life.

==Challenges and criticisms==

===Technology dependence===

Despite significant advances, Balisca remains dependent on imported technology in certain sectors, particularly specialized manufacturing equipment and advanced semiconductors. Critics argue that greater self-sufficiency should be a priority, especially in strategic technologies. The government has responded with programs to develop domestic capabilities in critical areas while maintaining international trade relationships.

===Brain drain concerns===

Some analysts have expressed concern about the emigration of highly trained Baliscan scientists and engineers to countries offering higher salaries. While the absolute numbers remain relatively small, the loss of top talent to better-funded research institutions abroad has been identified as a challenge for Baliscan science policy. The government has responded with programs to retain talent and attract diaspora researchers, including competitive salaries, research funding, and quality-of-life improvements.

===Ethical debates===

The development of [[artificial intelligence]], [[biotechnology]], and other emerging technologies has sparked ongoing ethical debates within Balisca. Concerns include the potential for AI to be used for surveillance, the implications of genetic engineering for human identity, and the environmental impacts of new technologies. The [[Confederal Ethics Commission for Research]] plays a key role in addressing these concerns, establishing guidelines for responsible research and development.

These debates reflect Balisca's democratic approach to technology governance, in which citizens participate in decisions about technological development rather than leaving such decisions to experts or market forces. The ongoing nature of these debates is seen as a strength rather than a weakness, ensuring that technology development remains aligned with social values.

